You found a file without knowing where it was — that is what `find` is for.

`find` walks a whole directory tree and prints every path that matches a test:

    find . -name vault.bak         by exact name, here and below
    find . -name "*.bak"           by pattern (quote it so the shell keeps it)
    find / -name flag.txt 2>/dev/null   search everything, hide "denied" noise

On a real system this is how you locate config files, logs, backups — and, in
security work, interesting targets. The classic recon move builds on the last
lesson:

    find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null

That lists every setuid program (runs as its owner, often root) — the first
place an attacker looks for a way up.

